# SURU Custom Rules — Cryptomining (Stratum protocol) detection
# MITRE ATT&CK: TA0040 Impact / T1496 Resource Hijacking
#
# SID block: 9990000–9999999 (SURU project-local range; never overlaps ET Open)
#
# Each rule MUST be on a single physical line — Suricata requires it.
# Validation: suricata -T -S suru-coinmining.rules
#
# WHY A CUSTOM RULE (coverage complementarity):
#  - The DNS side is already covered by pfBlockerNG's SURU_NoCoin DNSBL feed
#    (docs/detection-coverage-map.md §1.3, T1496) — it blocks resolution of
#    known mining-pool domains. This rule adds the DIRECT-IP / non-DNS path:
#    a host that reaches a mining pool by hardcoded IP (no DNS to block) is
#    caught here on the wire by its Stratum protocol handshake.
#  [MISSING REFERENCE: current ET Open coinminer SID coverage not re-verified
#   this session — emerging-malware/emerging-policy may carry some coinminer
#   signatures. This rule targets the Stratum JSON-RPC method strings
#   specifically, which is a precise, protocol-level signature independent of
#   feed currency. Propose web search: "Emerging Threats coinminer stratum
#   ruleset SID coverage 2026" to confirm/complement.]
#
# Stratum is the dominant mining-pool protocol: the miner opens a TCP session and
# sends JSON-RPC `{"method":"mining.subscribe"...}` then `mining.authorize`.
# Requiring BOTH method strings (vs either alone) keeps FP near-zero — these
# literals do not co-occur in normal traffic.
# FP note: a legitimate, operator-sanctioned miner will also match — allowlist
# its source via the Suricata Pass List if intentional mining is permitted on
# this network.
#
# WHY FLOWBITS (not a single rule with two content matches): mining.subscribe and
# mining.authorize are SEPARATE JSON-RPC messages with server chatter
# (mining.notify / set_difficulty / extranonce) between them, so on a real
# session they routinely land in DIFFERENT stream-reassembly chunks. A single
# rule with `content;content;distance:0` (or even two unordered contents in one
# rule) evaluates relative/co-presence matching only WITHIN the current inspected
# chunk, so it silently MISSES any session where the two literals straddle a
# reassembly-chunk boundary (this router's stream.reassembly.toserver-chunk-size
# is 2560 bytes — LIVE-TESTED false negative beyond that gap, 2026-07-05).
# Flowbits are FLOW-scoped state that persists for the
# life of the flow, independent of the chunk window: rule 1 sets a flowbit on
# subscribe (and does not alert); rule 2 alerts on authorize only when that
# flowbit is set. Live-tested to fire across split packets, reversed order, and a
# 3000-byte gap. See docs/detection-coverage-map.md §1.2.

# ── Rule 9990004 — Stratum leg 1: mining.subscribe (sets flowbit, does not alert) ──
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SURU Cryptomining - Stratum subscribe leg 1 (TA0040/T1496)"; flow:established,to_server; content:"mining.subscribe"; flowbits:set,suru.mining.subscribe; flowbits:noalert; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9990004; rev:2; metadata:tactic TA0040, technique T1496, affected_product Any, deployment Perimeter, falsepositives Operator_sanctioned_mining_allowlist_the_source;)

# ── Rule 9990005 — Stratum leg 2: mining.authorize after a subscribe on the same flow (TA0040/T1496) ──
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SURU Cryptomining - Stratum mining-pool handshake (TA0040/T1496)"; flow:established,to_server; content:"mining.authorize"; flowbits:isset,suru.mining.subscribe; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9990005; rev:1; metadata:tactic TA0040, technique T1496, affected_product Any, deployment Perimeter, falsepositives Operator_sanctioned_mining_allowlist_the_source;)
